}}</ref>

The Brazil senior team did not play a match at the Estádio Mineirão again until a 3–0 win over [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] in a [[2018 FIFA World Cup qualification (CONMEBOL)|World Cup qualifier]] in November 2016.<ref>{{cite web|title=Brazil and Neymar pile on Argentina woe with impressive display|url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report?gameId=431584|access-date=29 March 2020|work=[[ESPN]]|date=10 November 2016}}</ref> 

Brazil and Germany's next meeting in a major tournament was two years later when their under-23 teams faced each other in the [[Football at the 2016 Summer Olympics – Men's tournament final|gold medal match]] of the [[2016 Summer Olympics]]' men's tournament at the Maracanã as Rio de Janeiro hosted the Games. Both opposing coaches — [[Rogério Micale]] for Brazil and [[Horst Hrubesch]] for Germany — downplayed the fact that the gold medal match was a rematch of the World Cup semi-final, but some fans viewed the Olympic final as a chance for revenge and redemption. Brazil won on penalties to win its first Olympic football gold medal after the match finished 1–1 after both normal time and extra time, with Neymar scoring the winning penalty.<ref>{{cite web|last=Magowan|first=Alistair|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/olympics/37147347|title=Brazil's footballers exorcise demons to lift nation|access-date=1 January 2023|date=21 August 2016|website=BBC Sport|publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation}}</ref> 

The next time Brazil and Germany faced each other again at senior level was a [[Exhibition game#Association football|friendly]] at the [[Olympiastadion (Berlin)|Olympiastadion]] in [[Berlin]] on 27 March 2018; Brazil won 1–0 on a first half goal from [[Gabriel Jesus]].<ref>{{cite web|first=Michael|last=Emons|title=Germany 0–1 Brazil|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/43549049|access-date=29 March 2020|work=[[BBC Sport]]|date=27 March 2018}}</ref> 

Almost twelve years after the match, on 14 June 2026, Germany would win another World Cup match by the same score, this time at their first match of the [[2026 World Cup]]  against [[Curaçao national football team|Curaçao]] at the [[NRG Stadium]] in [[Houston|Houston, Texas]], [[United States]].<ref name="germany_curacao_bundesliga_2026">{{cite web
 |url=https://www.bundesliga.com/en/bundesliga/news/germany-curacao-world-cup-7-1-houston-stadium-group-e-report-havertz-37814
 |title=Germany 7-1 Curacao: Havertz stars as four-time champions make emphatic World Cup start
 |website=Bundesliga
 |publisher=DFL Deutsche Fußball Liga
 |date=2026-06-14
 |language=en
 |access-date=2026-06-17
}}</ref>

In Brazil, the result, "7–1" ({{langx|pt|sete a um}}), has become a metaphor for a devastating and crushing defeat,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Waldron |first=Travis |last2=HuffPost |date=2018-07-01 |title='Every Day, A New 7-1': How Brazil's Worst World Cup Loss Became A National Meme |url=https://www.huffpost.com/entry/brazil-7-1-world-cup-loss-germany_n_5b38fc96e4b0f3c221a1be4b |access-date=2025-12-05 |website=HuffPost |language=en}}</ref> while "Goal for Germany" ({{langx|pt|gol da Alemanha}}) is used as an exclamation after a mishap.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.faz.net/aktuell/sport/fussball/brasilien-ein-jahr-nach-dem-7-1-im-wm-halbfinale-13691306.html |title=Brasilien feiert 365 Tage ohne deutsches Tor |first=David |last=Klaubert |publisher=[[Frankfurter Allgemeine Zeitung]] |newspaper=faz.net |date=8 July 2015 |access-date=2 February 2016 |language=de |trans-title=Brazil celebrates 365 days without a German goal }}</ref><ref name="clima_politico_deboche_7a1_expressoes_populares">{{cite web
 |url=https://www.estadao.com.br/esportes/futebol/clima-politico-e-deboche-brasileiro-tornaram-7-a-1-e-gol-da-alemanha-expressoes-populares/
 |title=Clima político e deboche brasileiro tornaram '7 a 1' e 'gol da Alemanha' expressões populares
 |website=Estadão
 |publisher=O Estado de S. Paulo
 |author=Robson Morelli
 |date=2018-07-08
 |language=pt-BR
 |access-date=2026-06-17
}}</ref>

The phrases  "Todo dia um novo 7 a 1" (Every day a new 7–1) and "Todo dia um 7 a 1 diferente" (Every day a different 7–1) became widely used in Brazil to symbolize that something judged to be very bad or negative just happened or is currently happening.<ref name="opiniao_todo_dia_7a1_diferente_2026">{{cite web
